<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_lr-circuits" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>LR Circuits</title>
				
	<assemblage component="implementation">
  <title>Implementation Notes</title>
  <p>
	The activities in this section are designed to be implemented in an interactive classroom alongside the activities in <xref ref="sec_rc-circuits"/>.
	</p>
  </assemblage>

  <activity>
	<title>Exploring LR Circuits</title>
	<introduction>
		<p></p>
	<p>
		The circuit below contains a battery, a resistor, and an inductor.  The switch closes at <m>t = 0</m>.
	</p>
		<figure xml:id="x_fig_RL">
			<title>LR Circuit Diagram</title>
    		<caption>A circuit with a battery, a resistor, and an inductor.</caption>
    		<image source="LR-circuit1.png" width = "60%" ><description>A circuit diagram with a battery, open switch, resistor, and inductor in series.</description></image>
		</figure>
	</introduction>
	<task>
	<statement>
		<p>
			Is the initial current clockwise, counterclockwise, or zero?
		</p>
	</statement>
	<solution>
		<p>
			<xref ref="a_inductor-continuity"/> requires that the current be continuous, and it is zero right before the switch is closed, so it must be zero right after the switch is closed.
		</p>
	</solution>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Describe how the current changes in time using a physical argument.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Identify the largest current that flows through the circuit in terms of the given variables.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Sketch a graph of the current as a function of time.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
</activity>

	<subsubsection><title>Derivation: <q>Charging</q> Inductor</title></subsubsection>


	<p>
		Similar to an RC circuit, you can use the <xref ref="loop-rule"/> to relate the voltages across the different circuit elements: 
		<me>V_{bat} + V_R + V_L = 0</me>
		<me>V - I(t)R - L\frac{dI}{dt} = 0</me>
		This differential equation is directly analogous to the RC-charging circuit, with a solution given by:
		<me>I(t) = \frac{V}{R} \left(1 - e^{-tR/L}\right)</me>
	</p>

<activity>
	<title>Making Sense of LR Circuits</title>
	<statement>
		<p></p>
	
	<p>
		Make sense of the inductor <q>charging</q> equations in at least two different ways
	</p>
	</statement>
	<hint>
		<p>
			Good sensemaking techniques to try:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<p>
					What happens as <m>t \rightarrow \infty</m>?
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Give a physical interpretation for the quantity <m>\frac{L}{R}</m>.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					What happens as the voltage of the battery increases?
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Graph the current as a function of time.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ul>
	</hint>
		
</activity>

<activity>
	<title>LR Switch Circuit</title>
	<introduction>
		<p></p>
	<p>
		The circuit from before now contains a switch allowing the inductor to be disconnected from the battery.  Assume the switch has been flipped <em>up</em> for a long time.
	</p>
		<figure xml:id="x_fig_RL2">
			<title>LR Circuit Diagram</title>
    		<caption>A switched LR circuit.</caption>
    		<image source="LR-circuit2.png" width = "60%" ><description>A circuit diagram with a battery, open switch, resistor, and inductor in series.  A switch has been added, allowing a new branch with no battery (just an ideal wire) to replace the branch with the battery.</description></image>
		</figure>
	<p>
		After you flip the switch <em>down</em>:
	</p>
	</introduction>
	<task>
	<statement>
		<p>
			How does the current through the inductor compare to the current just before the switch is flipped?
		</p>
	</statement>
	<solution>
		<p>
			<xref ref="a_inductor-continuity"/> requires that the current be continuous, so it is still exactly the same as it was: <m>I = \frac{V}{R}</m>.
		</p>
	</solution>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Describe how the current changes in time using a physical argument.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Identify the current as <m>t \rightarrow \infty</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Sketch a graph of the current as a function of time.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
</activity>


  

	
<!--NO AI-->
</section>

